  "method": {
    "dogfood": "python3 benchmarks/dogfood-recall/run.py --axil <axil> --snapshot <per-model snapshot> --fetch 10. Each model's snapshot is snap-prefix with its .vec rebuilt by crates/engines/axil-vector/examples/swap_model.rs: the same 1,444 vector owners (232 memory records, 1,042 code proxies, 141 file summaries, 26 recall chunks, 3 checkpoints), each embedded with the new model from the text insert or its writer embeds; the 870 orphan vectors are dropped as heal --reindex drops them; axil.toml names the model. Rebuilt with bge-small it reproduces 1,443 of 1,444 stored vectors at cosine >= 0.99, and its eval differs from the untouched snapshot (control) on one question (q29, 3 -> 1), the orphans' effect seen on 2026-10-04.",
    "longmemeval": "longmemeval-bench --variant s --limit 20 --strategy recall-qtc --top-k 5 --model <m> --jobs 1; exact vector scan (per-question stores are far below the 20k threshold), one binary and one lockfile for all arms",
    "latency": "axil recall <q> --recall-format context-block --budget 2000 --top-k 5 (the prompt hook's call), one-shot process per question, the three snapshots interleaved question by question, one warm-up"
  },
  "recommendation": {
    "status": "final",
    "default": "keep bge-small-en-v1.5",
    "reasons": [
      "Dogfood (57 coding-memory questions, the CLI path agents use): the three models are within one or two questions of each other. NDCG@10 is 0.821 for bge-small, 0.820 for granite and 0.814 for gte. granite and gte each answer a net 2 more questions in the prompt hook's block (0.895 -> 0.930).",
      "LongMemEval s20 (single-session-user only): granite finds the same sessions as bge-small and ranks them better (NDCG@10 0.857 -> 0.912). gte finds one more (recall_all 0.90 -> 0.95, NDCG@10 0.919). That is one question type of six, 20 questions.",
      "Cost on this machine, every figure taken under load: granite takes about 4x bge-small's embedding time per stored session on LongMemEval and 3.6x per vector on dogfood; gte takes about 9x and 12x. The prompt hook's recall p50 is 1.2-1.3x bge-small's for granite and 3.5-3.8x for gte across two passes (load 10-27: 1,098 / 1,350 / 4,153 ms). gte's p50 is far past the hook's 1.8 s deadline, and granite's p90 (2,113 ms) crosses it where bge-small's (1,481 ms) does not.",
      "granite places unrelated text much closer than bge-small: the median same-table cosine is 0.80 vs 0.66, every pair clears the 0.65 conflict-candidate floor, and 1.8x as many pairs clear the 0.92 supersede threshold. Switching to it needs those thresholds re-tuned first, plus a re-embed, which no dimension check forces since both are 384-d.",
      "LongMemEval s60 (10 per question type, second machine): NDCG@10 0.946 for bge-small against 0.9369 for granite at equal hit rate; granite is ahead by more than 0.02 on single-session-user and behind by more than 0.02 on multi-session, temporal-reasoning, knowledge-update. The switch rule (more than 0.02 ahead across types, no lost hits) is not met. m5: equal NDCG@10 (0.9262).",
      "Cost on the second machine: granite's s60 took 2:56:52 against 1:18:47 with peak RSS 9.39 against 1.7 GiB; its m5 peaked at 18.92 GiB."
    ],
    "granite": "stays opt-in and experimental: the s60 run on the second machine does not show it ahead of bge-small, and it costs more time and memory",
    "gte-modernbert-base": "not as a default: its quality edge over granite on s20 is within one question, and it costs 2x (LongMemEval) to 3.4x (dogfood) granite's embedding time, a 2.2x larger vector store and a 572 MB model. Keep it registered (now with correct [CLS] pooling) for GPU users who want 768-d"
  },
